* ======================================================================
* account.entry.check  -  may this session enter this account?  ONE PLACE FOR THE RULE.
* In:  ent.name       the account name, lower case unless it is a path, as asked for
*      ent.suspended  true if its register record says SUSPENDED
* Out: ent.refused    true if the session may not enter it: the refusal is on the audit trail and server.error, error.msg and abort.message are set
*                     (the text is 10003 for every reason, so the caller cannot tell "no such account" from "not its account" from "suspended")
*
* 20 Oct 26 dm - REFACTOR, BEHAVIOUR PRESERVED (Solo carry 8; SD Core did the same on 18 Oct 26, fccf976).  These two tests were written out in vb.account
*   (request 3) and nowhere else, so request 29 (open an 'account;file' file) did not ask them at all.  Both requests call this now.  The text of the audit
*   lines and of the refusal is as it was.

account.entry.check:
ent.refused = @false

* 11 Sep 26 dm - A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT IS NOT ENTERED (PORT_ADOPTION 12; the
*   port's APISRVR of 27 Aug 2026).  The third door, after LOGIN and CPROC's
*   LOGTO; an API caller reaches an account through neither.
*
*   IT REUSES 10003, AND THAT IS THE RULE HERE.  Every refusal in this routine
*   gives the same text, so a caller cannot tell "no such account" from "not a
*   member" from "suspended", and the API does not enumerate the register for
*   anyone probing it.  No administrator exemption: an API session carries none.
if ent.suspended then
* 07 Oct 26 SD Core for Linux Solo (LSOLO 43, the full product's PAL-17) - the refusal is
*   on the audit trail, in Windows Solo's words (its ACCOUNT REFUSED branch=2), with
*   the account asked for appended.  The CALLER still sees only 10003.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'account refused branch=2 suspended account=' : ent.name)  ;* 07 Oct 26 PAL-24: the audit trail is lower case
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(10003) ;* User not allowed in requested account
   abort.message = error.msg
   ent.refused = @true
   return
end

* 29 Sep 26 SD Core for Linux Solo (LSOLO 6) - THE GROUP TEST IS GONE, AND
*   SOLO'S RULE REPLACES IT (SD Core Solo for Windows' "branch 4").  There are
*   no SD groups and no SDSYS exemption: the one enterable account is the one
*   the session holds - the name the SCRAM login proved, sduser - and SDSYS is
*   never a target (owner's ruling 11), whatever the transport.  The name is
*   read from the session (K$USERNAME), not from a client-supplied value.
acc.who = downcase(kernel(K$USERNAME, 0))
acc.member = (downcase(ent.name) # 'sdsys') and (downcase(ent.name) = acc.who)
if not(acc.member) then
* 07 Oct 26 SD Core for Linux Solo (LSOLO 43, PAL-17) - the line carries Windows Solo's
*   "branch=4" word, which it lacked.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'account refused branch=4 not.own.account user=' : acc.who : ' account=' : ent.name)
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(10003) ;* User not allowed in requested account
   abort.message = error.msg
   ent.refused = @true
end
return

* ======================================================================
* sdnet.account.check  -  request 29 (open an 'account;file' file): may THIS caller use THIS account's file?
* In:  net.acct       the account named in the request, as typed
* Out: net.refused    true if not allowed: server.error / status say so (set here)
*      net.acc.path   the account's directory, from its register record, when allowed
*
* 20 Oct 26 dm - Solo carry 8, SD Core's fccf976 (18 Oct 26).  The request used to ask only bit 2 of NETFILES.  With the bit set, ANY API login could open
*   the VOC-named file of ANY directory (sandbox-req29.py reads it back from the disk: another account's file, SDSYS's, one named by a path, a suspended
*   account's, one whose NET gate is off, and under SDCLIENT=1).  The account must now have a register record (a path names none, so nothing says who may
*   enter it), the session must be allowed to enter it by the rule request 3 applies (account.entry.check), and its NET gate - register field 15,
*   ACC$NET, ON only for the word yes - must be on.  SOLO'S DIFFERENCE FROM CORE: no SDSYS exemption from the gate, because SDSYS is never a target.

sdnet.account.check:
net.refused = @true
net.acc.path = ''
net.acct = downcase(net.acct)
net.rec = ''
net.found = @false
net.who = downcase(kernel(K$USERNAME, 0))
if net.acct # '' and index(net.acct, @ds, 1) = 0 then
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to net.f then
      read net.rec from net.f, net.acct then net.found = @true
      close net.f
   end
end
if not(net.found) then
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=29 reason=no register record user=' : net.who : ' account=' : net.acct)
   set.status ER$XREMOTE
   server.error = SV$ELSE
   return
end

ent.name = net.acct
ent.suspended = (upcase(trim(net.rec<ACC$SUSPENDED>)) = 'SUSPENDED')
gosub account.entry.check
if ent.refused then return

if not(upcase(trim(net.rec<ACC$NET>)) = 'YES') then
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'api refused request=29 reason=net gate off user=' : net.who : ' account=' : net.acct)
   set.status ER$XREMOTE
   server.error = SV$ELSE
   return
end

net.acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(net.rec<ACC$PATH>)
net.refused = @false
return

* ======================================================================
* SrvrAccount  -  Logto named account
* In:  account name

vb.account:
* Run the ON.LOGTO paragraph, if it exists

readv i from @voc,'ON.LOGTO',0 on error null
then execute 'ON.LOGTO' trapping aborts capturing response

new.account = upcase(cmnd)

old.account.path = account.path

if len(new.account) < 1 or len(new.account) > MAX.ACCOUNT.NAME.LEN then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5037) ;* Invalid account name
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - this took an account name or a pathname, and a
*   pathname reached a directory without going through the register at all.
*   Accounts are reached by name, as they are at LOGTO.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md
*   section 5.6.

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - register fields are read INSIDE the block where
*   acc.rec is known to be assigned, and initialised to '' first.  Reading
*   after the close would be reachable with acc.rec never set - an unassigned
*   reference is a compile-time WARNING and a run-time abort, the ERRGEN trap
*   in PROJECT_STATUS.md section 6.  (acc.group, read here for the Windows-group
*   test, went with it - SOLO 3 step 5.)
account.path = ''
acc.suspended = ''
* 20 Oct 26 SD Core Solo - RELEASE_1.1 165, THE OS SWITCH (Linux Solo 7c6bbfc, "api should read switches").  The account's SH and
*   OS.EXECUTE switches are read here from its register record, as LOGIN reads them at sign-on, and are set only once the account has
*   been entered (below), so a refusal or a revert leaves the previous account's switches with the previous account.  FAIL CLOSED:
*   both start off for every call, and an account with no record keeps them off.  Without this an API session would never hold either
*   switch, so an account given SH or OS over SET.OS.ACCESS would have neither over the API.
api.sh.grant = @false
api.os.grant = @false
api.net.grant = @false     ;* 25 Oct 26, Linux Solo 1f5f703 (SDNet carry 9; SD Core's S.65 slice 1): the NET gate (a file on another SD server), the same register field as request 29's
* 17 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 55.  ***EVERY REFUSAL HERE NAMES ITS
*   BRANCH ON THE AUDIT TRAIL, AND THAT IS KEPT RATHER THAN REMOVED WITH THE
*   DIAGNOSTICS IT ARRIVED WITH.***  All five refusals answer the caller with
*   the same sysmsg(10003) on purpose, so a client cannot tell "no such
*   account" from "not granted" from "suspended" - right against an attacker,
*   and it left an administrator with no way at all to find out why a login
*   was refused.  RELEASE_1.1 55 spent EIGHT CYCLES on a refusal that turned
*   out to be a failed group lookup, because nothing recorded which of the
*   five fired.  The trail is not the wire: it is readable by an
*   administrator and by nobody else, so naming the branch there costs the
*   attacker nothing and costs the operator nothing to have.
openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f then
   read acc.rec from acc.f, new.account then
      account.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)
      acc.suspended = acc.rec<ACC$SUSPENDED>
      api.sh.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$SH>)) = 'YES')
      api.os.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$OS.EXEC>)) = 'YES')
      api.net.grant = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$NET>)) = 'YES')
   end
   close acc.f
end

* 20 Oct 26 dm - THE RULE FOR ENTERING AN ACCOUNT (a suspended account is not entered; the account must be the session's own, SDSYS never; every refusal
*   is 10003 and audited) lives in account.entry.check, above, which request 29 asks as well.  Its comments are the old ones of this place.
ent.name = new.account
ent.suspended = acc.suspended
gosub account.entry.check
if ent.refused then goto revert.to.old.account

*   IT REUSES sysmsg(10003) LIKE EVERY OTHER REFUSAL IN THIS ROUTINE, which is
*   the rule here and not laziness - a caller cannot tell "no such account"
*   from "not granted" from "suspended" from this, so the API does not
*   enumerate the register's state for an attacker.  CPROC says more because a
*   local caller has already proved who they are.
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE TIER-ORDERING CHECK THAT STOOD
*   HERE IS GONE WITH THE TIERS.  The ACC$GROUP test above is the whole of this
*   routine's access rule now, and the identity note above it is why that is
*   enough on this route.

* 26 Sep 26 SD Core Solo - SOLO 3 step 5: the check.identity call that stood
*   here is gone with S4U.  It watched for a forked LocalSystem session losing
*   the user's impersonation; a Solo session IS the user by its process token
*   and has nothing to lose.

if not(ospath(account.path, OS$CD)) then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5019, account.path) ;* Unable to change to directory %1
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

openpath "voc" to new.voc else
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5163) ;* Directory is not a SD account
   goto revert.to.old.account
end

i = ospath("", os$flush.cache)

account.path = ospath("", os$cwd)     ;* Ensure @PATH is absolute path
initial.account.path = account.path   ;* Make WHO show only one element
who = new.account
void kernel(K$SET.ACCOUNT, who)   ;* 14 Oct 26 - the user table's account (LISTU, sheet 24 and 29)

voc = new.voc

* 0452 Set up the private catalogue pathname

private.catalogue = 'cat'
read voc.rec from voc, "$PRIVATE.CATALOGUE" then
   if upcase(voc.rec[1,1]) = 'X' then private.catalogue = voc.rec<2>
end

i = kernel(K$PRIVATE.CATALOGUE, private.catalogue)

* 20 Oct 26 SD Core Solo - RELEASE_1.1 165, THE OS SWITCH (Linux Solo 7c6bbfc): every way out of this routine that keeps the new account has
*   passed; load its switches now (see where they were read, above), BEFORE MASTER.LOGIN and the account's own LOGIN paragraph run, so
*   those run under the account's switches and not the previous ones.  The administrator flag is not touched: an API session carries none,
*   so the owner over the API is held to the account's own switches like anybody else.
void kernel(K$SH,      api.sh.grant)
void kernel(K$OS.EXEC, api.os.grant)
void kernel(K$NET,     api.net.grant)   ;* 20 Oct 26, SDNet carry 9: loaded at every entry to an account, as SD Core's apisrvr loads it (the set ends the account scope)

* 19 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux d6a86a7 (sheet 170): the MASTER.LOGIN paragraph of the SDSYS VOC is run for an API session as CPROC
*   runs it for an interactive or phantom one: once, when the session first enters an account, before that account's own LOGIN.  There is
*   no such paragraph unless the administrator wrote one.  It runs in the account just entered, under that account's switches.
if not(master.login.done) then
   master.login.done = @true
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'voc' to master.f on error null
   then
      read master.rec from master.f, 'MASTER.LOGIN' on error null
      then
         if upcase(master.rec[1,2]) = 'PA' then
            execute field(master.rec, @fm, 2, 9999999) trapping aborts capturing master.out
         end
      end
      close master.f
   end
end

* Run the LOGIN paragraph, if it exists

* 18 Aug 26 Windows port - AS TYPED, THEN LOWER, PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.12 (b).
*   @voc is a DYNAMIC file, so this is an exact byte match; without the second
*   attempt an API session would silently stop running the LOGIN paragraph the
*   moment the VOC id moves.  Blocked THEN/ELSE deliberately - see 5.12 trap 3.
readv i from @voc, 'LOGIN', 0 on error null
then
   execute 'LOGIN' trapping aborts capturing response
end else
   readv i from @voc, 'login', 0 on error null
   then
      execute 'login' trapping aborts capturing response
   end
end

return

revert.to.old.account:
if not(ospath(old.account.path, OS$CD)) then
   server.error = SV$ERROR
   error.msg = sysmsg(5162) ;* Fatal error : Cannot return to previous account
   return to abort.vbsrvr
end

* 13 Aug 26 Windows port - put @PATH back with the directory.  This restored
*   the one and not the other, so a refused account switch left @PATH holding
*   the path it had failed to reach.

account.path = old.account.path

return

* ======================================================================
* Open file
* In:  fname
* Out: fileno (short integer)

vb.open:
if kernel(K$SDNET, -1)  or config("SDCLIENT") # 0 then
   abort.message = sysmsg(5262) ;* File access is disabled
   server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   return
end

* Locate a spare file table entry

n = inmat(files)

for i = 1 to n
   if not(fileinfo(files(i), FL$OPEN)) then goto vb.open.continue
next i

* Extend file table

dim files(n + 50)
i = n + 1

vb.open.continue:
* Open file to file table entry i

* 20 Oct 26 dm - SDNet carry 9 (SD Core's S.65, 18 Oct 26): "dict <file>" in ANY case is a dictionary, opened as OPEN 'DICT', <file>.  The single-argument
*   OPEN looks the VOC for a file named "dict <file>", which _voc_ref understands only with an upper case DICT; a client's SDNet handler (SD Core's, and now Solo's)
*   sends it in lower case, so a remote dictionary was never opened (ELSE 3007) and COUNT, LIST, SORT and SELECT on a remote file stopped at "Cannot open
*   dictionary".  Only the word and the one space are taken off: the name goes to the same OPEN, so every refusal that applies to a file applies to the dictionary.
if upcase(cmnd[1,5]) = 'DICT ' then
   open 'DICT', trim(cmnd[6,9999]) to files(i)
   on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   then
      if bitand(fileinfo(files(i), FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.TRUSTED) then
         server.error = SV$ELSE
      end else
         response = iconv(i, 'ISL')
      end
   end
   else server.error = SV$ELSE
end else
   open cmnd to files(i)
   on error server.error = SV$ON.ERROR
   then
      if bitand(fileinfo(files(i), FL$FLAGS), FL$FLAGS.TRUSTED) then
         server.error = SV$ELSE
      end else
         response = iconv(i, 'ISL')
      end
   end
   else server.error = SV$ELSE
end
* 12 Nov 26 SD Core Solo - SD Core's S.73 item 3 (SOLO 56 group 8): the name the file was opened under, for request 70 (a verb run on the server names its file by it, never by what the caller sends).
if server.error = SV$OK then file.names<i> = cmnd

      return

      * ======================================================================
      * Close file
      * In:  fileno (short integer)
      * Out:

vb.close:
      fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
      if fno > SEQ.BASE then            ;* a sequential file (sheet 441; SOLO 56 group 7)
         gosub check.seq
         if err then return
         close seqfiles(si)
         seq.state(si) = 0
         return
      end
      gosub check.file
      if err then return

      close files(fno)   ;* 0346
      file.names<fno> = ''   ;* 12 Nov 26, SD Core's S.73 item 3 (SOLO 56 group 8)

      return

      * ======================================================================
      * Read record
      * In:  fileno (short integer)
      *      id
      * Out: record

vb.read:
      fno = oconv(cmnd[1,2], 'ISL')
      gosub check.file
      if err then return

      id = cmnd[3,99]
